# Environment for running the Startup Atlas MCP server over HTTP yourself
# (npm run mcp:http). The stdio server (npm run mcp) needs none of this.
#
# The server does NOT read this file automatically — copy the values into
# your shell or your hosting platform's environment settings:
#
#   export GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID='…'
#   export GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET='…'
#   …
#
# Full reference: docs/MCP-HTTP.md (variable table in "Run the server").
# Never commit real values; .env* is gitignored except this example.

# Required: Google OAuth client (Web application) from Google Cloud
# Console, with authorised redirect URI <your origin>/oauth/callback.
# On hosts that strip GOOGLE_* variables (DeepHost handler mode) use the
# ATLAS_GOOGLE_* names instead — they take precedence there.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Required for the OAuth flow: the public origin the server runs at,
# no trailing slash. Defaults to http://127.0.0.1:<port> when unset.
ATLAS_HTTP_BASE_URL=https://mcp.example.com

# Required: comma-separated Google accounts allowed to call any tool.
# Unset or empty allows nobody (fail-closed).
ATLAS_GOOGLE_ALLOWED_EMAILS=owner@example.com

# Optional: comma-separated accounts allowed to use the write tools
# (the server must also run with --write, or ATLAS_MCP_WRITE_ENABLED=true
# on handler-mode hosts).
ATLAS_GOOGLE_WRITE_EMAILS=owner@example.com

# Optional: enables the atlas_create_issue tool. Fine-grained GitHub
# token with Issues: Read and write on this repository only.
# ATLAS_GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Optional: bind settings. PORT (set by container platforms) wins over
# ATLAS_HTTP_PORT; ATLAS_HTTP_HOST=127.0.0.1 keeps the server local-only.
# ATLAS_HTTP_PORT=3000
# ATLAS_HTTP_HOST=127.0.0.1
